predictor_prune_microbench_2026-05-17
================================================================

Issue: #23 chunk 1 (multi-day predictor-pruning port)
Git commit at run time: 457279014fe5e1bc5ff65197f251e6654f692db9 (parent of
                        chunk-1 commit; bench measures the *primitive* in
                        isolation, not production wiring).
Host: AMD Ryzen 9 7950X (Linux 6.6, WSL2), 128 GB RAM (water-cooled, no
                        thermal throttling per CLAUDE.md global rules).

Command used to produce this file:
  cargo bench -p jxl-encoder --bench predictor_prune_microbench \
              --features __bench_internals 2>&1 \
              | tee benchmarks/predictor_prune_microbench_2026-05-17.txt

What is measured
----------------
Two strategies for the modular tree-learning root predictor selection:

  unconditional - call full `compute_predictor_entropy` for every one of
                  14 candidate predictors. This mirrors the production
                  code path in `jxl-encoder/src/modular/tree_learn.rs`
                  (`find_best_predictor`, lines ~4859-4948).

  with_lb_skip  - for each candidate, first compute the extra-bits-only
                  lower bound (the primitive shipped in chunk 1:
                  `modular::predictor_prune::predictor_extra_bits_lower_bound`).
                  Skip the full evaluation when `lb >= best_cost_so_far`.

Three sample counts mirror the dedup-strategies bench grid and the e7
real-photo profile (2026-05-16 lossless_cliff_profile):
  200K   ~ 0.26 MP image
  1.35M  ~ 1.05 MP image
  3.2M   ~ 4.19 MP image

Synthetic predictor cost profile: predictor 0 (Weighted-shaped) is best;
later candidates have monotonically higher mean extra-bits and flatter
histograms. This shape reflects what we see on real CLIC photos —
Weighted typically wins at the root and most of the other 13 candidates
have lower bounds at or above the Weighted total cost.

Parity guarantee (printed to stderr before benches run):
  best_pred chosen by `with_lb_skip` MUST equal the one chosen by
  `unconditional`, and `best_cost` bit-patterns MUST match. Asserted
  inside `print_parity_and_prune_stats`. No false skips.

Results summary
---------------
Prune rate (skipped predictors / 14):
  n=200K:   11/14 = 78.6 %
  n=1.35M:  11/14 = 78.6 %
  n=3.2M:   12/14 = 85.7 %

Wall-clock speedup on `find_best_predictor` (mean):
  n=200K:   1.3 ms → 0.9 ms   (-22.8 %, +35 % throughput)
  n=1.35M:  7.9 ms → 5.9 ms   (-25.3 %, +34 % throughput)
  n=3.2M:  19.0 ms → 12.9 ms  (-31.8 %, +47 % throughput)

Scaling: speedup widens with N because the histogram-build dominates
`compute_predictor_entropy` and the extra-bits-only lower bound is roughly
half the bytes touched. At N=3.2M, eliding 12 of 14 full evaluations
shaves ~6 ms per root call — meaningful at e9 where the root call is
on the multi-million-sample post-dedup set.

Chunk 2 plan (NOT in this commit)
---------------------------------
Wire the primitive into the sequential branch of
`find_best_predictor` (`tree_learn.rs:4878-4894` and the
`cfg(not(parallel-tree-learning))` mirror at `:4937-4945`). The parallel
branch (lines ~4900-4920) needs a different approach (the futures
materialize all costs before reducing); chunk 2 will sketch a
shared-atomic-best variant if the parallel-path savings are worth it.
The byte-identical-bitstream invariant is hash_lock_features.rs (36/36)
plus the corpus_regression test.
